[~chia7712] Yea that was my bad. We set the config in 
{code:java}
StreamsBuilder#build(Properties) {code}
and in the constructor of KafkaStreams.

[~lianetm] Thanks for your comments!
It helps me a lot!

I agree with keeping the logical groups as ERROR and RECONCILIATION. Based on your explanation and the current 6 BackgroundEvent.Type values, that split seems appropriate. My initial POLL_ONLY_RECONCILIATION vs CALLBACK_ANYTIME split was mainly trying to highlight the unsubscribe problem, where assignment events must not be applied. But as you pointed out, revoked/assigned events can belong to the same reconciliation flow in poll, and unsubscribe has a special need to avoid applying assignments. So splitting the logical groups into POLL_ONLY_RECONCILIATION / CALLBACK_ANYTIME does not seem right. My bad :)

I also agree that keeping the logical groups clear is more important than adding a broad abstraction. One smaller option could be to make the current skipAssignmentEvents flag explicit. Today, skipAssignmentEvents is effectively a context hidden behind a boolean, and understanding when it applies requires checking the processBackgroundEvents call sites. Instead, we could replace it with an internal consumer-only processing mode.

For example:

{code:java}
  boolean processBackgroundEvents(BackgroundEventProcessingMode processingMode) {
      ...
      if (processingMode == BackgroundEventProcessingMode.POLL) {
          events = backgroundEventHandler.drainForPoll();
      } else if (processingMode == BackgroundEventProcessingMode.UNSUBSCRIBE) {
          events = backgroundEventHandler.drainForUnsubscribe();
      }
      ...
  }{code}

The important point is that this processing mode would not introduce new logical groups. The logical groups would remain ERROR and RECONCILIATION. The mode would only define how the consumer drains/processes those groups in a specific API context. For unsubscribe, drainForUnsubscribe() could complete pending assignment events exceptionally and return only the reconciliation events that should actually be processed by the application thread.

This keeps the existing processBackgroundEvents loop mostly intact, removes the ambiguous skipAssignmentEvents boolean, and moves the assignment filtering/completion detail into BackgroundEventHandler rather than leaving it at the AsyncKafkaConsumer call sites.

What do you think?

